The documentation provides a countdown example demonstrating recursive snippet rendering:
```svelte
{#snippet blastoff()}..{/snippet}
{#snippet countdown(n)}
  {#if n > 0}
    <span>{n}...</span>
    {@render countdown(n - 1)}
  {:else}
    {@render blastoff()}
  {/if}
{/snippet}
{@render countdown(10)}
```
This shows how snippets can call themselves or other snippets recursively.

The documentation shows typing snippets using the `Snippet` type from 'svelte':
```typescript
import type { Snippet } from 'svelte';
interface Props {
  data: any[];
  children: Snippet;
  row: Snippet<[any]>;
}
```
You can also use generics to create more type-specific snippet definitions, ensuring type safety for component props.

The documentation shows two approaches for handling optional snippet props:
1. Using optional chaining:
```svelte
{@render children?.()}
```
2. Using an #if block with fallback content:
```svelte
{#if children}
  {@render children()}
{:else}
  fallback content
{/if}
```

Yes, the documentation states that snippets can reference values declared outside themselves, such as in the `<script>` tag or in other blocks. For example:
```svelte
<script>
let { message = `it's great to see you!` } = $props();
</script>
{#snippet hello(name)}
<p>hello {name}! {message}!</p>
{/snippet}
```
Snippets are 'visible' to everything in the same lexical scope.

Yes, the documentation confirms that snippets can reference themselves and each other. However, they must be within the same lexical scope. For example:
```svelte
{#snippet x()}
  {#snippet y()}...{/snippet}
  <!-- this is fine -->
  {@render y()}
{/snippet}
```
Outside the scope, attempting to render these snippets would result in an error.

