The documentation suggests using $state and $derived to make calculations reactive. For example, instead of a static calculation, you can use:
```typescript
let wordCount = $state(data.content.split(' ').length);
let estimatedReadingTime = $derived(wordCount / 250);
```
This ensures the calculation updates when the underlying data changes.

In Svelte's legacy mode, event handlers are declared using the `on:` directive. They can be declared inline with no performance penalty. For example:
```svelte
<script lang="ts">
let count = 0;
function handleClick(event: MouseEvent) {
  count += 1;
}
</script>
<button onclick={handleClick}>
  count: {count}
</button>
```
Event handlers can also be defined inline directly in the template.

Yes, Svelte allows multiple event listeners for the same event. For example:
```svelte
<script lang="ts">
let count = 0;
function increment() {
  count += 1;
}
function log(event: MouseEvent) {
  console.log(event);
}
</script>
<button onclick={increment} onclick={log}>
  clicks: {count}
</button>
```
In this example, both `increment` and `log` functions will be called when the button is clicked.

The most generic form of the `on` function accepts a generic `EventTarget`, a string event type, a generic `EventListener`, and optional configuration options:
```typescript
function on(
  element: EventTarget,
  type: string,
  handler: EventListener,
  options?: AddEventListenerOptions | undefined
): () => void;
```

When $state is used with arrays or simple objects, it creates a deeply reactive state proxy. This means modifying individual properties will trigger UI updates. For example:
```svelte
let todos = $state([
  {
    done: false,
    text: 'add more todos'
  }
]);

// This will trigger updates
[0].done = !todos[0].done;

// This will also create a proxied object
todos.push({
  done: false,
  text: 'eat lunch'
});
```

Use $state.snapshot() to take a static snapshot of a deeply reactive proxy, which is useful for passing state to external libraries or APIs that don't expect a proxy:
```svelte
<script>
let counter = $state({ count: 0 });
function onclick() {
  // Will log `{ count: ... }` rather than `Proxy { ... }`
  console.log($state.snapshot(counter));
}
</script>
```

State declared with $state cannot be mutated; it can only be reassigned. Instead of modifying object properties or using array methods like push, you must replace the entire object or array:
```svelte
let person = $state.raw({
  name: 'Heraclitus',
  age: 49
});

// This will have no effect
person.age += 1;

// This will work, creating a new person object
person = {
  name: 'Heraclitus',
  age: 50
};
```

The $state rune can be used in both public and private class fields, with the compiler transforming it into get/set methods:
```svelte
class Todo {
  done = $state(false);
  text = $state();

  constructor(text) {
    this.text = text;
  }

  reset() {
    this.text = '';
    this.done = false;
  }
}
```

A SvelteKit adapter function follows this basic structure:
```javascript
/** @param {AdapterSpecificOptions} options */
export default function adapt(options) {
  /** @type {import('@sveltejs/kit').Adapter} */
  const adapter = {
    name: 'adapter-package-name',
    // Implementation methods
  };
  return adapter;
}
```

To mount a Svelte component, use the `mount` function from Svelte. Here's an example:
```javascript
import { mount } from 'svelte';
import App from './App.svelte';

const app = mount(App, {
  target: document.querySelector('#app'),
  props: { some: 'property' }
});
```
Note that this mounts the component to the specified target element and allows mounting multiple components per page.

The key differences are:
- `mount` creates a new component and attaches it to the target element
- `hydrate` is used for server-side rendering and makes existing HTML interactive

Both methods have similar syntax:
```javascript
// Mount
const app = mount(App, {
  target: document.querySelector('#app'),
  props: { some: 'property' }
});

// Hydrate
const app = hydrate(App, {
  target: document.querySelector('#app'),
  props: { some: 'property' }
});
```
Importantly, effects will not run during these methods.

To unmount a component, use the `unmount` function on a component created with `mount` or `hydrate`:
```javascript
// after mounting
unmount(app);
```
This function is used to remove a previously mounted component from the DOM.

The `render` function is used for server-side rendering and returns HTML for different parts of the document:
```javascript
import { render } from 'svelte/server';
import App from './App.svelte';

const result = render(App, {
  props: { some: 'property' }
});

result.body;   // HTML for <body> tag
result.head;   // HTML for <head> tag
```
This allows you to generate HTML on the server side that can later be hydrated on the client.

Yes, the documentation explicitly states that you can mount multiple components per page. For example:
```javascript
// First component
const app1 = mount(App1, { target: document.querySelector('#app1') });

// Second component
const app2 = mount(App2, { target: document.querySelector('#app2') });
```
This allows for flexible component composition within a single page.

The `mount` and `hydrate` functions typically take two parameters:
1. The component to mount (e.g., `App`)
2. An options object with two main properties:
   - `target`: The DOM element to mount the component to
   - `props`: Any initial properties to pass to the component

```javascript
const app = mount(App, {
  target: document.querySelector('#app'),
  props: { some: 'property' }
});
```

The `hydrate` function is used to make server-rendered HTML interactive:
```javascript
import { hydrate } from 'svelte';
import App from './App.svelte';

const app = hydrate(App, {
  target: document.querySelector('#app'),
  props: { some: 'property' }
});
```
This method will reuse HTML previously rendered by Svelte's server-side rendering and make it interactive on the client side.

The documentation mentions you can mount components dynamically, such as creating a tooltip and attaching it to a hovered element. While no specific code example is provided, the `mount` function allows for this kind of dynamic component creation:
